{
  "dom_id": "step-q104170",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "$$\nr(u,v)=\u27e8u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}\u27e9\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-{z}^{2}=1$\nfor $z>0.$ What is the third component of $r?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z>0.$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=\u27e8x,\\,y,\\,z\u27e9=\u27e8x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1}\u27e9.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=\u27e8u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}\u27e9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that ${u}^{3}+{v}^{3}-1\\ge 0.$",
